--TEST--
`Core\Db\Column::type` answers a `Core\Db\ColumnType` case, so a program branches on something the backends agree about rather than on the vendor type name `getColumnMeta` hands back — `rule:core-classes/db-column-types`, spec § 18
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Db\ColumnType
var $db = Core\Db::connect("main");

foreach ($db->query("select id, payload from events", [])->columns() as Core\Db\Column $column) {
    // The whole reason the enum exists: a case is comparable against a case
    // this program wrote, which no vendor spelling is — `int4`, `INT`, `int`
    // and `bigint` are four backends' names for one of these.
    Core\Db\ColumnType $declared = $column->type();
    bool $whole = $declared == Core\Db\ColumnType::Int;

    // And the split its own docs are written around: a case describes what the
    // column was *declared* as and never what a read of it produces, so a
    // `JSON` column and a `TEXT` one are told apart here although `rule:core-classes/db-column-types`
    // reads both back as a `tainted string`.
    bool $json = $column->type() == Core\Db\ColumnType::Json;
    bool $text = $column->type() == Core\Db\ColumnType::Text;

    // The other two readers of the same class, so that the one refusal below
    // is the only thing in this case that does not compile.
    string $label = $column->name();
    bool $nullable = $column->nullable();
}

// The bound on the other side, which is what makes this an enum rather than
// the string PHP answers: there is no vendor name to read out of it, so a
// program that wants one has to say which backend's it means.
foreach ($db->query("select id from events", [])->columns() as Core\Db\Column $one) {
    string $vendor = $one->type();
}
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `string`, found `Core\Db\ColumnType`
   --> case.nvs:29:22
